Umm...no one else has said it so I will. The algorithm does things like this ALL THE TIME. You posted one hour of one day that you're video was promoted more than it as before, and you're claiming to be the all knowing algorithm guru. 

&amp;#x200B;

But if you were? You would know that things like this are common. People always complain about their videos not getting suggested but guess what? They are, they're just not getting a million views like they want. You know what's most likely happening in this image? The algorithm is testing your video on a larger scale. It's a machine and it needs to learn about your video through user behavior. It received some from your subscribers already. It's now serving it to people who are like them, and it may or may not pass the test. Just because your subscribers like this video doesn't mean the people like them will. It's now going to collect more data on the user patterns of this new group, and see what they do. Did they click? How long did they watch? Did they subscribe? Did they just keep scrolling past it on their home page? You see the 600 or so people who clicked it, but you don't have access yet to the data to know that 100,000 people were actually showed your thumbnail and the other 90,400 kept scrolling, lowering your click through rate and the algorithm determining that that video isn't for those people. I'm not saying that's the case but it very well could be, you just won't have access to that data yet.

&amp;#x200B;

You know what happens if it doesn't pass this algorithmic test? It drops right back down again to where it was before almost never to be promoted again because the algorithm determined that your video was bad. Remember it can't watch videos, it has to take millions of data points about user behavior to determine whether or not your video is good. And it takes time for it to process that data. The one thing it does know? What people watch and don't watch. 

&amp;#x200B;

So I'm happy for you and that feeling is awesome I know, but you need to look for a pattern over the next few days first before you get all excited and start giving out high and mighty advice on the process. Post another screen shot in a few days to let us all know how that algorithmic test worked out for you. This post is a few hours old now, and who knows it might have plummeted already or it might even be being served to even more people who are also like the sample size (they watch and enjoy the same content).

&amp;#x200B;

I have a video that's doing the exact same thing now, only mines been doing so over the past week. It's passed the tests it's been given, because it's being served to more and more people. I keep getting comments from people asking why that video kept following them around everywhere, because that's what the algorithm does, it makes a calculated guess that that person will watch that video, so it follows them. And weird, then they do, and comment, and watch more of my content increasing their session time. I can even tell where they're from based off the peak views and time of day, it's huge in India and the Philipines when I'm sleeping. Views while I snooze.

&amp;#x200B;

So congrats again dude, but just wait a little longer before you claim your channel is now a huge success.

Yes, it's exactly what it means. Stop thinking that YouTube is the one watching and recommending videos when in fact it's PEOPLE. People are the ones who decide what's popular and what's not and the algorithm just follows them. It watches what they do. Did they click? Did they keep scrolling? Did they watch and how long? Did they watch other videos by that creator or someone else? Or did they just go back to whatever site they came from?

&amp;#x200B;

Everyone always complains about the algorithm holding them down, when in fact 100% of the time it's their content. Argue it if you want but it's the truth. You're not doing anything different, at all. Again I get it man, I did that.  I was making men's lifestyle videos wondering why I wasn't getting views on them. Well the problem was I was making videos of which YouTube had already indexed 33 million video results. Why should they promote \*my\* video on being more confident when there are 502 million other videos about the exact same thing? So I finally decided to do something DIFFERENT in a niche that not a lot of guys are in and it's been SUPER easy to stand out. I'm in a niche that has a ton of demand but not a lot of supply. I wish I would have made the switch a year ago as opposed to making crappy men's lifestyle videos that were basically a clone of everything Alpha M and dudes like that were doing...

&amp;#x200B;

PEOPLE get tired of seeing the same thing over and over (psychological hacks to get her to notice you, how to be an Alpha male, etc). And they don't click or watch. And then the algorithm just follows...

I don't know where you learned that from but it isn't true at all. YouTube doesn't take into consideration how many videos you have when determining whether or not to surface them. That being said though, that's awesome that you have a social media following already and are confident that your video will do well but...then what? And you don't get to determine whether or not your video is high quality. The audience determines that. Hell, we all think our content is \*high quality\*... 

&amp;#x200B;

People are going to see your video and enjoy it and go to your channel to watch more and there won't be anything else there. So they will either go watch something else or leave YouTube all together, the latter of which is really bad. Your video will then be known as a video that ends sessions and it won't promote it nearly as much as other videos. I STRONGLY suggest you create  a bunch of content first before diving into something big like that, as it will be much more beneficial then. Just my two cents.

Your audience ISN'T on Reddit, they're on YOUTUBE! So Why "promote" here?
Here's what's up. I get it, I was totally that guy, I thought that my content was great and that all it needed was exposure. I found a bunch of subreddits that allowed me to post my content and it got me a TON of views...and absolutely nowhere with my channel. I'll explain why in a second. If I see one more "how do I promote my content" thread...

&amp;#x200B;

I've been doing this for two years now, and if I had to start over I'm damn near confident that I would be crushing it right now. It took me two damn years to niche down and admit to myself that everything about my channel sucked. Hell there's one of you who said (DMx...something? you're a reaction channel, sorry for arguing with you lol) that my thumbnails were bad (comment below if that was you). At first I was kinda mad but then I was like "wait...they do...they're awful". I finally figured out how the algorithm works and SEO and best practices and all of that. Finally. 

&amp;#x200B;

Now you need to understand something, and it's something that it took me forever to learn, your audience isn't ANYWHERE but YouTube. Not Reddit, not Instagram, not Twitter. You can post your content there and get  a TON of views, but views mean nothing to the algorithm. It's all about what they do NEXT. Did they subscribe? Keep watching other videos? Watch someone else's content? Oooor did they watch and then LEAVE YouTube?

&amp;#x200B;

 I would post my content on different Subreddits and get like 30K views overnight, and then my video would be dead for MONTHS and I couldn't figure out why. Well...here's why. People on different platforms and what the algorithm is looking for in good content are two COMPLETELY different things. Like polar opposites. When you share a video on Reddit people might watch for 30 seconds. And it's not because they don't like it or hate self promotion. They're in there car, or at work, or standing in line at Target.  What do they do after they watch your video? You might say that they dig deep into your content and start binging but no, they don't. At least not very often, like at all. They don't stay on YouTube, they go back to where they came from. People don't like to leave whatever platform they're comfortable with. I never leave Reddit. I don't use Instagram for anything but a companion to my YouTube channel. It doesn't interest me. And a lot of people on Instagram never even TOUCH Reddit. I don't start binge sessions from Reddit, I start them when I'm ready to. 

&amp;#x200B;

All of that goes in direct opposition to what the algorithms (there are about 6) are looking for. They want people to come to YouTube and STAY ON YOUTUBE! They want to promote videos that keep people watching for long periods of time. LET THE ALGORITHM FIND YOUR AUDIENCE FOR YOU! That's literally it's job. Now if you're small and complaining that "YouTube isn't giving you exposure" you're absolutely wrong. Go check your impressions, they're showing your content all over the place. When people say "they aren't getting exposure" they're really saying "I'm not getting 100k views per video like I want". Plain and simple.

&amp;#x200B;

Niche down so that you can make it easy for the algorithms to figure out who your content is for. It's literally a machine that has one goal, match content to the right person at the right time. The easier you make it's job the faster you will grow. Have a variety channel?  It's not out to get you. It's not out to hold you down and keep the big channels up. It doesn't give a shit. I've seen better growth on my channel in the last month than I have in two damn years. All because I give the algorithms what the want, binge sessions with creative watch time traps. Have six similar videos? Put them in a playlist sure, but put them ALL in the description AND the pinned comment. Make it so people have to TRY and avoid your content. I see so many people come into a video and bounce around in the comments. You know what the algorithm does then? It looks for more people like them with similar watch history and throws my videos on their homepage. Like clock work every time. 

&amp;#x200B;

I love Reddit and spend WAY TO MUCH time on here, but forget about posting links on here thinking it's going to get you 500k subscribers.
